<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Riyadh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Riyadh is the capital and largest city of Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a> region of central Arabia. A small oasis town, it was taken by the First Saudi State in 1773 and became the capital of the second Saudi state under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turki-ibn-abdullah">Turki ibn Abdullah</a> in 1824. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Abd al-Aziz ibn Saud</a>'s recapture of it from the Rashidis in 1902 began the making of modern Saudi Arabia, and oil wealth turned it into a city of millions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/riyadh.jpg" alt="Riyadh" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: B.alotaby · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Riyadh_Skyline.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the fifth century Abikarib As'ad and his son Hassan Yuha'min left an inscription at Ma'sal al-Jumh, west of modern <b>Riyadh</b>, recording an expedition into central Arabia, and the Kingdom of Kinda served as Himyar's client among the Arab tribes of the interior.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In September 2014 the Houthis, tacitly aided by forces loyal to Saleh, seized Sanaa; Hadi fled to Aden and then to <b>Riyadh</b>, and in March 2015 a Saudi-led coalition intervened.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Republic of Yemen · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1902 Abdul Rahman's son Abdulaziz, later Ibn Saud, recaptured <b>Riyadh</b> and founded the third Saudi state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The emirate expanded by war and conversion, taking <b>Riyadh</b> in 1773, defeating the Banu Khalid Emirate and conquering al-Hasa.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-diriyah">Emirate of Diriyah</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhammad ibn Rashid, emir from 1869 to 1897, intervened in the Saudi civil war, took <b>Riyadh</b> in 1887 and won at Mulayda in 1891, becoming master of central Arabia.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-jabal-shammar">Emirate of Jabal Shammar</a></li><li><blockquote>Six years after the Ottoman-Egyptian destruction of the Emirate of Diriyah, Turki ibn Abdullah, a grandson of Muhammad ibn Saud, drove the Egyptian garrison from <b>Riyadh</b> in 1824 and made it his capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-nejd">Emirate of Nejd</a></li></ul></section>
